milestones.text:
A line can be perfectly formatted and still be wrong. Try `withdraw bob 80` when Bob has 50: `*balance -= amount` on a `u64` panics with `attempt to subtract with overflow`. The type system stopped a negative balance, but crashing the bank isn't the right answer either. These are **business rules**, and each needs a clear error:

| Problem | Message |
|---|---|
| depositing or withdrawing 0 | `amount must be positive` |
| an account that doesn't exist | `no account named "carol"` |
| opening an account twice | `account "alice" already exists` |
| withdrawing more than the balance | `insufficient funds: bob has 50, needs 80` |

They're printed like parse errors (`line 3: insufficient funds: bob has 50, needs 80`), and a rejected transaction changes nothing. Opening an account with 0 is allowed: empty accounts are normal.

### A Bank type that guards its own rules

Wrap the map in a struct and give it methods that return `Result`:

```rust
struct Bank {
    accounts: BTreeMap<String, u64>,
}

impl Bank {
    fn balance_mut(&mut self, name: &str) -> Result<&mut u64, String> { ... }
    fn open(&mut self, name: &str, amount: u64) -> Result<String, String> { ... }
    fn deposit(&mut self, name: &str, amount: u64) -> Result<String, String> { ... }
    fn withdraw(&mut self, name: &str, amount: u64) -> Result<String, String> { ... }
    fn apply(&mut self, tx: &Transaction) -> Result<String, String> { ... }
}
```

`balance_mut` is where "no such account" is decided, once: `self.accounts.get_mut(name).ok_or_else(|| format!(...))` turns the `Option` into a `Result` with a message. Every other method just writes `let balance = self.balance_mut(name)?;`.

`apply` matches on the transaction and calls the right method. It takes `&Transaction` because applying a transaction doesn't need to own it (that will matter in the last milestone).

Now both steps return `Result<_, String>`, so one small function can chain them with `?`:

```rust
fn process(bank: &mut Bank, line: &str) -> Result<String, String> {
    let tx = parse_line(line)?;
    bank.apply(&tx)
}
```

`main` calls `process` and prints either the message or `line N: error`. Notice that `main` no longer cares *which* step failed: a parse error and a business-rule error travel the same road. That's the payoff of using one error type throughout.

Error messages follow the Rust convention: lowercase, no final period, and the values that help (who, how much). Someone reading `insufficient funds: bob has 50, needs 80` at 2 a.m. knows exactly what happened.

