Your `main` now reads input, counts, ranks and prints, all in one long function. Before adding the last feature, give the program the shape real tools have: **compute first, print last**.

- `struct Stats` holds every number the report needs: `lines`, `words`, `chars`, `bytes`, `sentences` and the `counts` map.
- `fn analyze(text: &str) -> Stats` does all the counting and doesn't print anything.
- Small helpers answer one question each: `fn top_words(counts: &BTreeMap<String, usize>, n: usize) -> Vec<(&String, &usize)>`, `fn longest_words(...)`, `fn average_length(...) -> f64`.
- `fn print_report(stats: &Stats)` only formats and prints.

Why bother? Because now each piece can be used and tested on its own. A web version of this tool would call `analyze` and render HTML instead of calling `print_report`. A test can check `analyze("a b a").counts` without capturing any output. Separating computation from presentation is one of the most useful habits in software.

### New: sentences, and empty input

Add a `sentences:` line after `bytes:`. A sentence ends at `.`, `!` or `?`, but only if some text came before it, so `Wait... what?!` is 2 sentences, not 5. Text after the last end mark (no final period) counts as one more sentence. A simple way: walk the characters with a flag that says "we're inside a sentence".

```rust
let mut sentences = 0;
let mut in_sentence = false;
for c in text.chars() {
    if c == '.' || c == '!' || c == '?' {
        if in_sentence {
            sentences += 1;
            in_sentence = false;
        }
    } else if c.is_alphanumeric() {
        in_sentence = true;
    }
}
```

(and one more if `in_sentence` is still true at the end).

Finally, handle text with no words at all. Right now the average divides 0 by 0 and prints `NaN`, which is the kind of output that makes users stop trusting a tool. If there are no words, print the five count lines and then `no words found` instead of the rest. For the input `...!` that's:

```
lines: 1
words: 1
characters: 5
bytes: 5
sentences: 0
no words found
```

