```

```compiler
error[E0499]: cannot borrow `lexer` as mutable more than once at a time
```

The elision rule says: with `&mut self`, the output borrows from `self`. So `first` keeps the lexer mutably borrowed, and you can't ask for a second word while holding the first. Writing `-> &'a str` tells the compiler the truth: the word points into the *source text*, which outlives any one call. With that fix the program prints `let x`.

