milestones.text:
An order is enough to run tasks one by one. But `compile` and `docs` don't depend on each other, so a machine with several cores could run them **at the same time**. Group the tasks into **waves**: a task goes in the wave right after the last of its dependencies. Tasks with no dependencies form wave 1:

```text
wave 1: fetch
wave 2: configure
wave 3: compile, docs
wave 4: test
wave 5: package
```

Everything in a wave can run in parallel, because all of its dependencies finished in earlier waves. Computing the waves is one pass over the topological order: a task's wave number is 1 + the largest wave number among its dependencies (and those are already known, because they come earlier in the order). List each wave's tasks in name order.

### Running a wave on threads

The "work" here stands in for compiling or testing: a deterministic hash that takes time proportional to the cost. Copy this function into your program exactly, so your results match the tests:

```rust
/// Stands in for real work (compiling, testing, packaging): a hash of the task's name that
/// takes time proportional to `cost`. The same task always gives the same result.
fn work(name: &str, cost: u32) -> u64 {
    let mut hash: u64 = 0xcbf2_9ce4_8422_2325;
    for _ in 0..cost * 1000 {
        for byte in name.bytes() {
            hash ^= byte as u64;
            hash = hash.wrapping_mul(0x0100_0000_01b3);
        }
    }
    hash
}
```

For each wave, print `wave N: ...`, run every task of the wave on its own thread, wait for all of them, and then print each result as 16 hex digits (`{hash:016x}`), in the wave's (name) order:

```text
wave 3: compile, docs
  compile: 6473b0d92f53a73d
  docs: bb4fc9d65872bbe5
```

`std::thread::scope` is the right tool: threads spawned inside the scope may **borrow** local data (here, the task map and the names), because the scope guarantees they all finish before it returns:

```rust
let hashes: Vec<u64> = thread::scope(|s| {
    let handles: Vec<_> = wave.iter().map(|&name| s.spawn(move || work(name, tasks[name].cost))).collect();
    handles.into_iter().map(|h| h.join().expect("a task thread panicked")).collect()
});
```

### Deterministic output from parallel code

Threads finish in whatever order the operating system likes. If each thread printed its own result, the lines would come out in a different order from run to run, which is maddening to read and impossible to test. So threads only **compute**; the main thread prints, after joining, in a fixed order. Joining the handles in the order they were spawned gives you the results in that same order no matter which thread finished first. Collect, then print: remember that rule for every concurrent program you write.

