**E0597** means a reference would still be used after the value it points to has been **dropped**. If the program were allowed to run, the reference would point at freed memory (a **dangling reference**). The fix is always one of: make the value live longer, make the reference live shorter, or store an owned value instead of a reference.

## The basic case: an inner scope

```rust-error
fn main() {
    let longest: &str;
    {
        let name = String::from("Ferris");
        longest = &name;
    }
    println!("{longest}");
}
```

```compiler
error[E0597]: `name` does not live long enough
 --> src/main.rs:5:19
  |
4 |         let name = String::from("Ferris");
  |             ---- binding `name` declared here
5 |         longest = &name;
  |                   ^^^^^ borrowed value does not live long enough
6 |     }
  |     - `name` dropped here while still borrowed
7 |     println!("{longest}");
  |                ------- borrow later used here
```

The labels show the timeline: where the value is declared, where it's borrowed, where it's dropped (the closing brace), and where the borrow is used after that. Fixes: declare `name` in the outer scope, or make `longest` an owned `String` (`longest = name;` moves it out).

## Storing references to temporaries in a loop

```rust-error
fn main() {
    let mut labels: Vec<&str> = Vec::new();
    for i in 0..3 {
        let label = format!("item-{i}");
        labels.push(&label);
    }
    println!("{labels:?}");
}
```

```compiler
error[E0597]: `label` does not live long enough
```

Each `label` is dropped at the end of its loop iteration, but the vector keeps a reference to it. rustc notes "`labels` is a collection that stores borrowed references, but `label` does not live long enough to be stored in it". The fix is to let the vector **own** the strings: `Vec<String>` and `labels.push(label)`.

This is the most common E0597 at work: a struct or collection typed with references (`&str`, `&T`) that is filled with values created on the fly. When the data is created inside the loop or function, the container must own it.

## Returning a reference to a local

Returning `&s` where `s` is a local `String` gets its own error code, **E0515** ("cannot return reference to local variable `s`"), and a signature like `fn make() -> &str` with no reference parameters gets **E0106**. Same idea, same fix: return the owned `String`.

## Threads and `'static`

Passing a reference to a local into something that needs `'static` data, like `thread::spawn` or `tokio::spawn`, reports E0597 with "argument requires that `x` is borrowed for `'static`" (or E0373 for closures). Move owned data into the thread with `move`, share it with `Arc`, or use `thread::scope` so the threads end before the data does.

## How to think about it

Ask: **who owns this data, and is that owner still alive when I use the reference?** References are for temporary access to data someone else owns for longer. If nobody owns it for long enough, own it yourself.

topics.example:
use std::thread;

fn make_greeting(name: &str) -> String {
    format!("Hello, {name}")
}

fn main() {
    // Fix 1: the owner lives in the outer scope.
    let name = String::from("Ferris");
    let longest: &str;
    {
        longest = &name;
    }
    println!("{longest}");

    // Fix 2: the collection owns its strings.
    let mut labels: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
    for i in 0..3 {
        let label = format!("item-{i}");
        labels.push(label);
    }
    println!("{labels:?}");

    // Fix 3: return owned data from functions.
    println!("{}", make_greeting("Corro"));

    // Fix 4: scoped threads may borrow, because they end before the data does.
    let totals = vec![3, 4, 5];
    let sum = thread::scope(|s| s.spawn(|| totals.iter().sum::<i32>()).join().unwrap());
    println!("sum {sum} of {totals:?}");
}

fn longest<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str {
    if a.len() >= b.len() { a } else { b }
}

fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str {
    s.split_whitespace().next().unwrap_or("")
}

fn default_name() -> &'static str {
    "guest"
}

fn shout(s: &str) -> String {
    s.to_uppercase()
}

struct Excerpt<'a> {
    text: &'a str,
}

impl<'a> Excerpt<'a> {
    fn first_sentence(&self) -> &'a str {
        self.text.split('.').next().unwrap_or(self.text)
    }
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", longest("arena", "crab"));
    println!("{}", first_word("ownership rules everything"));
    println!("{}", default_name());
    println!("{}", shout("borrow checker"));

    let book = String::from("Call me Ferris. Some years ago I learned Rust.");
    let e = Excerpt { text: &book };
    println!("{}", e.first_sentence());
}

struct Job {
    name: String,
    result: Option<String>,
}

impl Job {
    fn name(&self) -> &str {
        &self.name
    }

    fn peek(&self) -> Option<&str> {
        self.result.as_deref()
    }

    fn take_result(&mut self) -> Option<String> {
        self.result.take()
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut names = vec![String::from("Ferris"), String::from("Corro"), String::from("Gopher")];
    let first = &names[0];
    println!("borrowed {first}");
    let copy = names[1].clone();
    let taken = std::mem::take(&mut names[2]);
    println!("copy {copy}, taken {taken}, left behind {:?}", names[2]);
    let removed = names.remove(0);
    println!("removed {removed}, now {names:?}");

    let mut job = Job { name: String::from("backup"), result: Some(String::from("ok: 42 files")) };
    println!("{} peek {:?}", job.name(), job.peek());
    let out = job.take_result();
    println!("took {:?}, now {:?}", out, job.peek());
}

use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use serde_json::json;

#[derive(Debug, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]
struct Order {
    order_id: u64,
    customer: String,
    items: Vec<Item>,
    #[serde(default)]
    gift_wrap: bool,
    #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
    coupon: Option<String>,
}

#[derive(Debug, Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct Item {
    sku: String,
    qty: u32,
    price_cents: u64,
}

fn main() {
    let input = r#"{
        "orderId": 1042,
        "customer": "Ferris",
        "items": [
            {"sku": "MUG-01", "qty": 2, "price_cents": 1250},
            {"sku": "TEE-XL", "qty": 1, "price_cents": 2000}
        ]
    }"#;

    let order: Order = serde_json::from_str(input).expect("valid order JSON");
    let total: u64 = order.items.iter().map(|i| u64::from(i.qty) * i.price_cents).sum();
    println!("order {} for {}: {} items, total {total} cents, gift wrap {}", order.order_id, order.customer, order.items.len(), order.gift_wrap);
    println!("first sku {}", order.items[0].sku);

    println!("{}", serde_json::to_string(&order).unwrap());

    for bad in [r#"{"orderId": 1}"#, r#"{"orderId": "x", "customer": "a", "items": []}"#, "<html>502</html>"] {
        match serde_json::from_str::<Order>(bad) {
            Ok(o) => println!("parsed {:?}", o.coupon),
            Err(e) => println!("error: {e}"),
        }
    }

    let event = json!({ "type": "checkout", "order": order.order_id, "ok": true });
    println!("{} {}", event, event["order"].as_u64().unwrap_or(0));
}

use anyhow::{Context, Result, bail, ensure};
use thiserror::Error;

// A "library" error: callers can match on it.
#[derive(Debug, Error)]
enum StoreError {
    #[error("item `{0}` not found")]
    NotFound(String),
    #[error("only {available} left, asked for {requested}")]
    OutOfStock { requested: u32, available: u32 },
    #[error("invalid quantity")]
    BadQuantity(#[from] std::num::ParseIntError),
}

fn reserve(stock: &[(&str, u32)], item: &str, qty_text: &str) -> Result<u32, StoreError> {
    let requested: u32 = qty_text.trim().parse()?; // ParseIntError -> StoreError via #[from]
    let (_, available) = stock.iter().find(|(name, _)| *name == item).ok_or_else(|| StoreError::NotFound(item.to_string()))?;
    if requested > *available {
        return Err(StoreError::OutOfStock { requested, available: *available });
    }
    Ok(available - requested)
}

// "Application" code: anyhow plus context.
fn handle_order(stock: &[(&str, u32)], line: &str) -> Result<String> {
    let (item, qty) = line.split_once(':').context("expected `item:qty`")?;
    ensure!(!item.is_empty(), "empty item name");
    if item == "banned" {
        bail!("item `{item}` cannot be sold");
    }
    let left = reserve(stock, item, qty).with_context(|| format!("processing order {line:?}"))?;
    Ok(format!("reserved {item}, {left} left"))
}

fn main() {
    let stock = [("mug", 5), ("tee", 1)];
    for line in ["mug:2", "tee:3", "hat:1", "mug:two", "banned:1", "no colon"] {
        match handle_order(&stock, line) {
            Ok(msg) => println!("ok    {msg}"),
            Err(e) => {
                println!("error {e:#}");
                if let Some(StoreError::OutOfStock { available, .. }) = e.downcast_ref::<StoreError>() {
                    println!("      (could offer {available} instead)");
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, PartialOrd)]
struct Version {
    major: u64,
    minor: u64,
    patch: u64,
}

fn parse(v: &str) -> Option<Version> {
    let mut parts = v.split('.').map(|p| p.parse::<u64>().ok());
    let (major, minor, patch) = (parts.next()??, parts.next()??, parts.next()??);
    Some(Version { major, minor, patch })
}

// Cargo's default (caret) requirement: same leftmost non-zero number, and not older.
fn caret_allows(req: &str, candidate: &str) -> bool {
    let (Some(r), Some(c)) = (parse(req), parse(candidate)) else {
        return false;
    };
    let compatible = if r.major > 0 {
        c.major == r.major
    } else if r.minor > 0 {
        c.major == 0 && c.minor == r.minor
    } else {
        c.major == 0 && c.minor == 0 && c.patch == r.patch
    };
    compatible && c >= r
}

fn main() {
    let checks = [
        ("1.4.2", "1.4.9"),
        ("1.4.2", "1.9.0"),
        ("1.4.2", "2.0.0"),
        ("1.4.2", "1.4.1"),
        ("0.4.2", "0.4.7"),
        ("0.4.2", "0.5.0"),
        ("0.0.3", "0.0.4"),
    ];
    for (req, candidate) in checks {
        let verdict = if caret_allows(req, candidate) { "allowed" } else { "rejected" };
        println!("{:<9} {candidate:<6} {verdict}", format!("\"{req}\""));
    }
    println!("debug build: {}", cfg!(debug_assertions));
}

// Idiomatic versions of the "before" patterns from the table above.
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn shout(s: &str) -> String {
    s.to_uppercase()
}

fn total(values: &[i32]) -> i32 {
    values.iter().sum()
}

fn first_long(words: &[&str]) -> Option<usize> {
    words.iter().find(|w| w.len() > 4).map(|w| w.len())
}

fn main() {
    let names: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
    if names.is_empty() {
        println!("no names yet");
    }

    let scores = vec![3, 9, 4];
    for (i, s) in scores.iter().enumerate() {
        println!("#{i}: {s}");
    }
    println!("total {}", total(&scores));

    let greeting = String::from("hello");
    println!("{}", shout(&greeting));

    let mut counts: HashMap<&str, u32> = HashMap::new();
    for word in ["a", "b", "a"] {
        *counts.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;
    }
    let mut keys: Vec<_> = counts.iter().collect();
    keys.sort();
    println!("{keys:?}");

    let empty: Option<Vec<i32>> = None;
    println!("{:?} {:?}", empty.unwrap_or_default(), first_long(&["hi", "crabby"]));
}

use std::collections::BTreeMap;

macro_rules! square {
    ($x:expr) => {
        $x * $x
    };
}

// Variadic: zero or more key => value pairs, optional trailing comma.
macro_rules! btreemap {
    ($($key:expr => $value:expr),* $(,)?) => {{
        let mut map = BTreeMap::new();
        $( map.insert($key, $value); )*
        map
    }};
}

// Several rules, tried in order.
macro_rules! max_of {
    ($x:expr) => { $x };
    ($x:expr, $($rest:expr),+) => {{
        let a = $x;
        let b = max_of!($($rest),+);
        if a > b { a } else { b }
    }};
}

// Generating items: one function per identifier.
macro_rules! unit_fns {
    ($($name:ident => $factor:expr),* $(,)?) => {
        $( fn $name(v: f64) -> f64 { v * $factor } )*
    };
}

unit_fns! {
    km_to_miles => 0.621371,
    kg_to_pounds => 2.20462,
}

// stringify! turns the caller's tokens into text.
macro_rules! check {
    ($cond:expr) => {
        println!("{:<22} {}", stringify!($cond), if $cond { "ok" } else { "FAILED" });
    };
}

fn main() {
    println!("{} {}", square!(7), square!(2 + 3));
    let stock = btreemap! { "potion" => 5, "sword" => 1, "arrow" => 40, };
    println!("{stock:?}");
    println!("max {}", max_of!(3, 17, 9, 12));
    println!("{:.2} miles, {:.1} lb", km_to_miles(10.0), kg_to_pounds(5.0));
    check!(square!(4) == 16);
    check!(stock.len() > 5);
}

/// Splits a slice into two mutable halves at `mid`, like the standard `split_at_mut`.
fn split_halves(values: &mut [i32], mid: usize) -> (&mut [i32], &mut [i32]) {
    let len = values.len();
    assert!(mid <= len, "mid {mid} out of range for length {len}");
    let ptr = values.as_mut_ptr();
    // SAFETY: mid <= len was checked above, so both ranges are inside the slice,
    // and [0, mid) and [mid, len) don't overlap, so the two &mut never alias.
    unsafe { (std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr, mid), std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr.add(mid), len - mid)) }
}

unsafe extern "C" {
    // The C standard library's abs(), linked into every Rust program on common platforms.
    fn abs(input: i32) -> i32;
}

fn c_abs(x: i32) -> i32 {
    // SAFETY: abs has no preconditions for values other than i32::MIN.
    if x == i32::MIN { i32::MAX } else { unsafe { abs(x) } }
}

fn main() {
    let mut scores = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];
    let (left, right) = split_halves(&mut scores, 2);
    left[0] += 1;
    right[0] += 100;
    println!("{scores:?}");

    let x = 42;
    let p = &x as *const i32; // creating a raw pointer is safe
    // SAFETY: p points to x, which is alive and initialized.
    let value = unsafe { *p };
    println!("through raw pointer: {value}");

    println!("abs from C: {} {}", c_abs(-17), c_abs(5));
}

use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::io::{self, BufWriter, Write};

// Borrows its input and returns a count: no allocation per call.
fn count_errors(lines: &[&str]) -> usize {
    lines.iter().filter(|l| l.starts_with("ERROR")).count()
}

fn main() -> io::Result<()> {
    let log: Vec<String> = (0..1000).map(|i| if i % 7 == 0 { format!("ERROR job {i}") } else { format!("INFO job {i}") }).collect();
    let views: Vec<&str> = log.iter().map(|s| s.as_str()).collect();
    println!("errors: {}", count_errors(&views));

    // Reuse one buffer instead of allocating a new String per line.
    let mut buf = String::with_capacity(64);
    let mut longest = 0;
    for line in &log {
        buf.clear();
        buf.push_str(line);
        buf.make_ascii_uppercase();
        longest = longest.max(buf.len());
    }
    println!("longest line {longest} bytes, buffer capacity still {}", buf.capacity() >= 64);

    // HashSet membership instead of Vec::contains in a loop.
    let banned: HashSet<u32> = [7, 49, 343].into_iter().collect();
    let allowed = (1..=1000).filter(|n| !banned.contains(n)).count();
    println!("allowed ids: {allowed}");

    // Pre-sized Vec plus an iterator chain.
    let mut squares = Vec::with_capacity(10);
    squares.extend((1..=10u64).map(|n| n * n));
    println!("sum of squares {}", squares.iter().sum::<u64>());

    // Lock stdout once and buffer many writes.
    let stdout = io::stdout();
    let mut out = BufWriter::new(stdout.lock());
    for n in squares.iter().take(5) {
        write!(out, "{n} ")?;
    }
    writeln!(out)?;
    out.flush()?;
    Ok(())
}
